codomain Y .\nL73@P1: It is straightforward to verify that\nL74@P1: deg(f) = 1\nL75@P1: |X|\nL76@P1: X\nL77@P1: y∈Y\nL78@P1: |f\nL79@P1: −1\nL80@P1: (y)|\nL81@P1: 2\nL82@P1: ;\nL83@P1: this is the formulation that we will use in the remainder of the article. For brevity, we\nL84@P1: will often simply call deg(f) the “degree” of f. For a function f : X → Y , we necessarily\nL85@P1: have 1 6 deg(f) 6 |X|. The lower bound is attained if and only if f is injective, and\nL86@P1: the upper bound is attained if and only if f is a constant function. More generally, the\nL87@P1: degree of a k-to-1 map is precisely k. Notice that |X| deg(f) is the number of pairs\nL88@P1: (x, x0) ∈ X × X such that f(x) = f(x\nL89@P1: 0\nL90@P1: ). Equivalently, if x is chosen randomly from the\nL91@P1: uniform distribution on X, then deg(f) is the expected number of elements x\nL92@P1: 0 ∈ X such\nL93@P1: that f(x\nL94@P1: 0\nL95@P1: ) = f(x). If we define a probability distribution ν on X by ν(x) = |f\nL96@P1: −1\nL97@P1: (x)|/|X|,\nL98@P1: then log(|X|/ deg(f)) is the R´enyi entropy of ν of order 2. The quantity deg(f) − 1 was\nL99@P1: also termed the “coefficient of coalescence” of f in [1].\nL100@P1: In Section 2, we analyze the degrees of some specific families of discrete dynamical\nL101@P1: systems of combinatorial interest. The first types of systems are “sorting maps” that\nL102@P1: act on permutations of length n; these are the bubble sort map, the (West) stack-sorting\nL103@P1: map, and a map that we call “nibble sort” (which swaps the first pair of adjacent entries\nL104@P1: in a nonidentity permutation that are in decreasing order). We also consider analogues\nL105@P1-2: the electronic journal of combinatorics 27(3) (2020), #P3.51 2of bubble sort and nibble sort acting on words. Another type of system we consider is\nL106@P2: defined via chip-firing on a cycle graph and can be interpreted as a dynamical system on\nL107@P2: the set of binary strings of length n. Those last two types of systems act on partitions of\nL108@P2: a fixed positive integer n; these are the Bulgarian solitaire map and its close relative, the\nL109@P2: Carolina solitaire map.\nL110@P2: Section 3 focuses on comparing deg(f) with deg(f\nL111@P2: k\nL112@P2: ), where f\nL113@P2: k denotes the kth iter\u0002ate of f. The original motivation for doing this comes from a desire to show that the\nL114@P2: submultiplicativity condition deg(f ◦ g) 6 deg(f) deg(g) can fail horribly, even in the\nL115@P2: specific case in which f = g. For an explicit small example of this failure, consider the\nL116@P2: map f : {1, 2, 3} → {1, 2, 3} given by f(1) = 2 and f(2) = f(3) = 3, which satisfies\nL117@P2: deg(f) = 5/3 and deg(f\nL118@P2: 2\nL119@P2: ) = 3. We will prove that for every fixed positive integer k and\nL120@P2: real number γ > 2 − 1/2\nL121@P2: k−1\nL122@P2: , we have\nL123@P2: max\nL124@P2: f:X→X\nL125@P2: |X|=n\nL126@P2: deg(f\nL127@P2: k\nL128@P2: )\nL129@P2: deg(f)\nL130@P2: γ\nL131@P2: = Θ(n\nL132@P2: 1−1/2\nL133@P2: k−1\nL134@P2: ).\nL135@P2: Setting k = γ = 2 shows that the ratio deg(f\nL136@P2: 2\nL137@P2: )/ deg(f)\nL138@P2: 2\nL139@P2: can be as big as Θ(√\nL140@P2: n) (but no\nL141@P2: bigger).\nL142@P2: We collect several open problems and conjectures in Section 4.\nL143@P2: Before we proceed, let us mention the following more refined estimates for the degree,\nL144@P2: which will be useful in the remainder of the article.\nL145@P2: Lemma 2. If X and Y are finite sets and f : X → Y is a function, then\nL146@P2: |X|\nL147@P2: |f(X)|\nL148@P2: 6 deg(f) 6 max\nL149@P2: y∈Y\nL150@P2: |f\nL151@P2: −1\nL152@P2: (y)|.\nL153@P2: Proof. The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality tells us that\nL154@P2: deg(f) = 1\nL155@P2: |X|\nL156@P2: X\nL157@P2: y∈f(X)\nL158@P2: |f\nL159@P2: −1\nL160@P2: (y)|\nL161@P2: 2 >\nL162@P2: 1\nL163@P2: |X|\nL164@P2: 1\nL165@P2: |f(X)|\nL166@P2: \nL167@P2: \nL168@P2: X\nL169@P2: y∈f(X)\nL170@P2: |f\nL171@P2: −1\nL172@P2: (y)|\nL173@P2: \nL174@P2: \nL175@P