se that for all k, the number of elements of Y with k\nL236@P3: preimages under f equals the number of elements of Z with k preimages under g. This\nL237@P3: means that there exists a bijection h : Y → Z such that |f\nL238@P3: −1\nL239@P3: (y)| = |g\nL240@P3: −1\nL241@P3: (h(y))| for all\nL242@P3: y ∈ Y . For each y ∈ Y , there is a bijection αy : f\nL243@P3: −1\nL244@P3: (y) → g\nL245@P3: −1\nL246@P3: (h(y)), so that f(x) = y\nL247@P3: implies αy(x) ∈ g\nL248@P3: −1\nL249@P3: (h(y)) and g(αy(x)) = h(y) = h(f(x)). Now simply define eh : X → Y\nL250@P3: 0\nL251@P3: by eh(x) = αf(x)(x) for all x ∈ X. We readily check that g(eh(x)) = g(αf(x)(x)) = h(f(x))\nL252@P3: for every x ∈ X, so f and g are pseudoconjugate.\nL253@P3: It is immediate from the previous proposition that two pseudoconjugate maps must\nL254@P3: have the same degree.\nL255@P3: 2 Specific Discrete Dynamical Systems\nL256@P3: In this section, we consider specific families of discrete dynamical systems that are indexed\nL257@P3: by positive integers. In order to define these maps, we recall the following standard\nL258@P3: definitions.\nL259@P3: A permutation is an ordering of a finite set of positive integers. We view permutations\nL260@P3: as words. Let Sn be the set of permutations of the set [n] := {1, . . . , n}. The normalization\nL261@P3: of a permutation π = π1 · · · πn is the permutation in Sn obtained by replacing the i\nL262@P3: th\nL263@P3: -\nL264@P3: smallest entry in π with i for all i ∈ [n]. A descent of a permutation π = π1 · · · πn is an\nL265@P3: index i ∈ [n − 1] such that πi > πi+1. Let Des(π) be the set of descents of π. Given a\nL266@P3: tuple of positive integers a = (a1, . . . , ar), we define Wa to be the set of all words over\nL267@P3: the alphabet [r] that contain exactly ai copies of the letter i for all i. A composition of a\nL268@P3: positive integer n is a tuple c = (c1, . . . , c`) of positive integers that sum to n. The entries\nL269@P3: c1, . . . , c` are called the parts of c. A partition of a positive integer n is a composition of n\nL270@P3: whose parts appear in nonincreasing order. Let Comp(n) and Part(n) denote, respectively,\nL271@P3: the set of compositions of n and the set of partitions of n.\nL272@P3: 2.1 Bubble Sort and Its Iterates\nL273@P3: Suppose π is a permutation of length n and i ∈ [n − 1]. If i ∈ Des(π), let ti(π) be the\nL274@P3: permutation obtained from π by swapping the i\nL275@P3: th and (i + 1)st entries in π. If i 6∈ Des(π),\nL276@P3: let ti(π) = π. The operators t1, . . . , tn−1 play an important role in algebraic combinatorics\nL277@P3: because they generate the 0-Hecke algebra of the symmetric group Sn [12].\nL278@P3-4: the electronic journal of combinatorics 27(3) (2020), #P3.51 4Let B(π) = tn−1 ◦ tn−2 ◦ · · · ◦ t1(π). The function B is called the bubble sort map. An\nL279@P4: alternative recursive description of B is as follows. First, B sends the empty permutation\nL280@P4: to itself. If π is a permutation with largest entry m, then we can write π = LmR for some\nL281@P4: permutations L and R. Then B(π) = B(L)Rm. For example,\nL282@P4: B(416352) = B(41) 3526 = 143526.\nL283@P4: By a slight abuse of notation, we will use the same letter B to denote the restriction\nL284@P4: of B to Sn, which we can view as a discrete dynamical system on Sn. We refer the\nL285@P4: interested reader to [4] and [16, pages 106–110] for additional information and interesting\nL286@P4: properties of the bubble sort map. For instance, Knuth reports that for k 6 n − 1, the\nL287@P4: number of permutations of length n that are completely sorted after k iterations of B is\nL288@P4: (k + 1)n−k−1(k + 1)!; from our point of view, this is a formula for the cardinality of the\nL289@P4: k-fold preimage of the identity permutation.\nL290@P4: For every fixed positive integer k, we will give an exact formula for the degree of\nL291@P4: the iterate Bk: Sn → Sn. We first need the following preliminary definitions. Given a\nL292@P4: permut